<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Buddhagupta stone</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 5th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Buddhagupta inscription</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Buddhagupta stone is an inscribed tablet of about the 5th century CE found in 1834 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/province-wellesley">Seberang Perai</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a>, south of Kedah. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> inscription, in an early South Indian script, prays for a safe voyage for the master mariner (mahanavika) Buddhagupta of Raktamrittika, and it is carved with a stupa. It is among the earliest evidence of Buddhist Indian merchants in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/buddhagupta-stone.jpg" alt="Buddhagupta stone" width="320" height="385" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: L joo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ChiTu005.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
